# Opus / token discipline

Prefer small, scoped agent turns. Do not load the whole `_notes/cursor/` corpus into every chat.

## When Opus is worth it

Use Opus (or equivalent high-cost reasoning) for:

- Analytics correctness (`02-analytics-semantics.md`): session/trade-date, expected grid, OHLCV aggregation, rolling VWAP
- DQ engine design, scoring, reconciliation (`04-dq-rules-and-scoring.md`)
- Sticky bugs involving timezone, DST, session boundaries, or dual-frequency (`minute` vs `daily`)

Prefer cheaper Cursor models (Composer / Grok) for scaffolding, boilerplate routes, Streamlit chrome, stub tests, and mechanical edits — unless the user has already directed otherwise.

Avoid Opus fast mode unless the user asks for speed; standard rates are enough.

## Context budget

- Attach **only the note section(s)** needed for the current slice, plus the files being edited
- One concern per chat when possible (e.g. `CMP.*` only, or ingest only, or `/v1/analytics/vwap` only)
- Prefer `@` references to specific paths over dumping all of `_notes/cursor/`
- Where `06-sample-data.md` disagrees with earlier notes, `06` wins — but only open it when the claim is factual about the sample

Doc map for slices:

| Slice | Primary note |
|---|---|
| Domain / symbology | `01-futures-data-primer.md` |
| Bars, VWAP, grid | `02-analytics-semantics.md` |
| Schema / SQL | `03-data-model.md` |
| Rules, score, insights | `04-dq-rules-and-scoring.md` |
| Routes / payloads | `05-api-contract.md` |
| Sample facts / oracle tests | `06-sample-data.md` |

## Implementation shape

- Short plan before first app code; then implement one layer or rule family
- Small, testable units; pytest for quality rules and query helpers
- Do not re-litigate locked decisions in `_notes/cursor/README.md` unless the user asks
